WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.187
This slide explains how to use the module page effectively.

00:00:04.187 --> 00:00:06.537
First, read the concept on each slide.

00:00:06.537 --> 00:00:10.267
Try to understand the main idea before running any code.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:17.802
If the slide introduces a new term, such as module, package, class, object, method, or self,

00:00:17.802 --> 00:00:20.957
pause and say the idea in your own words.

00:00:20.957 --> 00:00:22.127
Second, run the code examples.

00:00:22.127 --> 00:00:26.372
The examples are small on purpose.

00:00:26.372 --> 00:00:30.162
They are designed so you can see one idea clearly at a time.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:35.152
Third, check the output. Do not just run the cell and move on.

00:00:35.152 --> 00:00:37.902
Look carefully at what Python prints.

00:00:37.902 --> 00:00:40.952
Ask yourself why that output makes sense.

00:00:40.952 --> 00:00:45.817
Fourth, edit the code. Change one value, one name, or one small expression.

00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:50.822
Then run the cell again and compare the result.

00:00:50.822 --> 00:00:57.462
Finally, apply the idea. Think about where the pattern would be useful in an analytics project.

00:00:57.462 --> 00:01:00.152
For example, imports help you use tools that already exist.

00:01:00.152 --> 00:01:04.937
Modules help you separate helper functions.

00:01:04.937 --> 00:01:08.692
Classes help you keep related data and behavior together.

00:01:08.692 --> 00:01:11.972
A good way to learn this module is to move slowly.

00:01:11.972 --> 00:01:16.160
Run the examples, make small edits, and explain what changed.
